registerChapter({
  id: 'swap',
  num: '30',
  phase: 'Phase 6 · Verification',
  title: 'swap',
  blurb: 'A four-command program with two temporaries, verified twice — once by composing rules and once from the semantics — with the two proofs measured against each other and a rule for choosing between them.',

  orient: {
    youWill: [
      'Write down the four assertions between the commands of a four-command program before writing any Lean, and say what forces each one.',
      'Prove that a load preserves a fact about a <i>different</i> variable, and point at the line where <code>tmp₁ ≠ tmp₂</code> is consumed.',
      'Say why the specification needs no hypothesis <code>l₁ ≠ l₂</code>, and extract that disequality from the precondition in one term.',
      'Isolate the effect of a program on the whole heap as a standalone equation, and prove it by splitting the address space into three regions.',
      'Assemble a four-step <code>Exec</code> derivation whose bracketing matches the program&rsquo;s, and use <code>show</code> to turn a 192-line goal display into a three-line one.',
      'Decide, on a program in front of you, whether to compose rules or drop to the semantics — and defend the choice with something other than length.'
    ],
    needs: [
      'Unit 29: the four moves, both load rules, <code>hoare_write_val</code>, <code>preserves_of_storeStable</code>, <code>pure_star_regroup</code>.',
      'Unit 27: <code>hoare_frame</code> and the fact that it cuts the <code>∗</code> at top level, taking the left conjunct as the small rule&rsquo;s footprint.',
      'Unit 24: <code>Preserves</code>, <code>HeapOnly</code>, <code>preserves_of_heapOnly</code>, <code>not_heapOnly_pure</code>.',
      'Units 05–09: <code>singleton_same</code>, <code>singleton_other</code>, <code>write_same</code>, <code>write_other</code>, <code>union_of_some</code>, <code>union_of_none</code>, <code>singleton_disjoint_iff</code>.'
    ],
    payoff: 'This is the last verification you drive by hand. Every assertion on this page is forced by the command after it, which means none of them was really a choice — and the next unit turns that observation into a definition that computes them from the postcondition backwards.'
  },

  blocks: [

    /* ==================================================== the program ==== */

    {t:'p', h:"Here is the program. Four commands, two temporaries, and not one construct in it you have not run before."},

    {t:'code', cap:'Two loads, then two writes. <code>;;</code> is <code>infixr</code>, so the brackets are there to be read rather than obeyed — they are what Lean would have inserted anyway.',
     src:"def swap (tmp₁ tmp₂ : Var) (l₁ l₂ : Loc) : Cmd :=\n  .load tmp₁ l₁ ;; (.load tmp₂ l₂ ;; (.write l₁ (.var tmp₂) ;; .write l₂ (.var tmp₁)))"},

    {t:'p', h:"And here is the specification. Before, the cell at <code>l₁</code> holds <code>a</code> and the cell at <code>l₂</code> holds <code>b</code>; afterwards they hold each other&rsquo;s contents. One hypothesis, <code>tmp₁ ≠ tmp₂</code>, and it is about the two program variables rather than about memory."},

    {t:'txt', cap:'The whole claim. Nothing about the store survives into the postcondition — the temporaries are scaffolding.',
     src:"theorem swap_spec (tmp₁ tmp₂ : Var) (l₁ l₂ : Loc) (a b : Val) (hne : tmp₁ ≠ tmp₂) :\n    Hoare ((l₁ ↦ a) ∗ (l₂ ↦ b)) (swap tmp₁ tmp₂ l₁ l₂) ((l₁ ↦ b) ∗ (l₂ ↦ a))"},

    {t:'p', h:"You are going to prove that, and you are going to decide how. This page gives you the plan in words, works the first rung in full, and supplies the one ingredient the course has not got. The rest is the three exercises, each with its own hint ladder — and between the first and the second there is a decision to make, which this page settles by measuring both answers rather than by asserting one."},

    {t:'p', h:"Start with what is <i>not</i> in the statement. There is no hypothesis <code>l₁ ≠ l₂</code>, and the program plainly needs the two addresses to differ: at one address the two writes collide, the second overwriting whatever the first put there, and nothing is swapped. So the specification had better not be claiming anything in that case — and it is not, because when <code>l₁ = l₂</code> no state satisfies its precondition. Unit 17 proved that as <code>two_cells_distinct</code>: owning two cells is already owning two <i>different</i> cells. The disequality is in the theorem — in its precondition rather than its hypotheses — and the proof will have to dig it out."},

    /* ============================================== the four assertions ==== */

    {t:'sec', s:'The four assertions'},

    {t:'p', h:"A four-command program needs four assertions: one after each command, the last of which is the postcondition. The way to find them is to read the program backwards. Take the last command, ask what its rule needs in front of it, and write that down; that is the assertion after the third command. Repeat. Nothing about the earlier commands enters the calculation — the assertion between two commands is a fact about the <i>second</i> one."},

    {t:'p', h:"Do that here and the four fall out. Write them in words first; there is nothing to gain by reaching for Lean before you know what you are claiming."},

    {t:'ol', items:[
      "After <code>tmp₁ := [l₁]</code>: both cells are still owned and unchanged — <code>l₁ ↦ a</code> and <code>l₂ ↦ b</code> — and <code>tmp₁</code> holds <code>a</code>.",
      "After <code>tmp₂ := [l₂]</code>: both cells still unchanged, and now two facts about the store, <code>tmp₁ = a</code> and <code>tmp₂ = b</code>.",
      "After <code>[l₁] := tmp₂</code>: the first cell has changed, so <code>l₁ ↦ b</code> and <code>l₂ ↦ b</code>. Of the two store facts only <code>tmp₁ = a</code> is still wanted; <code>tmp₂</code> has been spent and can be dropped.",
      "After <code>[l₂] := tmp₁</code>: <code>l₁ ↦ b</code> and <code>l₂ ↦ a</code>, and nothing about the store. That is the postcondition."
    ]},

    {t:'p', h:"Item 1 records a fact about <code>tmp₁</code> that nothing consumes until the fourth command. Between them sit two commands, and one of them — the second load — writes a program variable. So the plan has a step in it that the previous unit&rsquo;s toolkit cannot take: a fact about <code>tmp₁</code> has to be carried across a command that assigns to <code>tmp₂</code>. That is the whole of what is new here, and it arrives at rung two."},

    {t:'p', h:"One thing more has to be decided before the assertions can be written in Lean, and it is bookkeeping rather than mathematics. <code>hoare_frame</code> cuts the <code>∗</code> at top level and treats the <b>left</b> conjunct as the small rule&rsquo;s footprint. So each assertion has to be arranged with the next command&rsquo;s cell on the left and everything else on the right, which is why the four look lopsided:"},

    {t:'txt', cap:'The same four assertions, arranged for the rule that will consume each one. <code>φ₁</code> is <code>fun σ => σ tmp₁ = a</code> and <code>φ₂</code> is <code>fun σ => σ tmp₂ = b</code>.',
     src:"pre   (l₁ ↦ a) ∗ (l₂ ↦ b)\n\n  load tmp₁ l₁          footprint l₁ ↦ a\nA₁    (l₂ ↦ b) ∗ (pure φ₁ ∗ (l₁ ↦ a))\n\n  load tmp₂ l₂          footprint l₂ ↦ b\nA₂    (aAnd (fact φ₂) (l₁ ↦ a)) ∗ (pure φ₁ ∗ (l₂ ↦ b))\n\n  write l₁ (.var tmp₂)  footprint aAnd (fact φ₂) (l₁ ↦ a)\nA₃    (aAnd (fact φ₁) (l₂ ↦ b)) ∗ (l₁ ↦ b)\n\n  write l₂ (.var tmp₁)  footprint aAnd (fact φ₁) (l₂ ↦ b)\npost  (l₁ ↦ b) ∗ (l₂ ↦ a)"},

    {t:'p', h:"The two write rungs use <code>hoare_write_val</code>, whose precondition is <code>aAnd (fact …) (l ↦ old)</code> — the value to be written carried in the same assertion as the cell it goes into. That is why <code>A₂</code> and <code>A₃</code> have an <code>aAnd</code> on the left and a <code>pure</code> on the right: the fact the <i>next</i> command needs is glued to its cell, and the fact a <i>later</i> command needs travels separately, in the frame."},

    /* =================================================== rung one, full ==== */

    {t:'sec', s:'Rung one, in full'},

    {t:'p', h:"Split at the first <code>;;</code> and supply <code>A₁</code>. The four moves are the ones from the previous unit: split, frame, apply, renormalise."},

    {t:'trace', title:'The first command, three states',
     start:"tmp₁ tmp₂ : Var\nl₁ l₂ : Loc\na b : Val\nhne : tmp₁ ≠ tmp₂\n⊢ Hoare (l₁ ↦ a ∗ l₂ ↦ b) (swap tmp₁ tmp₂ l₁ l₂) (l₁ ↦ b ∗ l₂ ↦ a)",
     steps:[
       {tac:'refine hoare_seq (Q := (l₂ ↦ b) ∗ (pure (fun σ => σ tmp₁ = a) ∗ (l₁ ↦ a))) ?_ ?_',
        state:"case refine_1\n⊢ Hoare (l₁ ↦ a ∗ l₂ ↦ b) (Cmd.load tmp₁ l₁) (l₂ ↦ b ∗ (_root_.pure fun σ => σ tmp₁ = a) ∗ l₁ ↦ a)\n\ncase refine_2\n⊢ Hoare (l₂ ↦ b ∗ (_root_.pure fun σ => σ tmp₁ = a) ∗ l₁ ↦ a)\n    (Cmd.load tmp₂ l₂ ;; Cmd.write l₁ (Atom.var tmp₂) ;; Cmd.write l₂ (Atom.var tmp₁)) (l₁ ↦ b ∗ l₂ ↦ a)",
        h:"The four context lines are unchanged in both goals and are left off here. <code>swap</code> has unfolded: the first goal mentions one command and the second mentions the other three, which is the shape every rung has. The remaining program is <i>right</i>-nested, so the next <code>hoare_seq</code> will split it in the same place again."},
       {tac:'have step : … := hoare_frame (hoare_load tmp₁ l₁ a) (heapLocal_load tmp₁ l₁) (preserves_of_heapOnly _ (heapOnly_pointsTo l₂ b))',
        state:"case refine_1\nstep : Hoare (l₁ ↦ a ∗ l₂ ↦ b) (Cmd.load tmp₁ l₁) (((_root_.pure fun σ => σ tmp₁ = a) ∗ l₁ ↦ a) ∗ l₂ ↦ b)\n⊢ Hoare (l₁ ↦ a ∗ l₂ ↦ b) (Cmd.load tmp₁ l₁) (l₂ ↦ b ∗ (_root_.pure fun σ => σ tmp₁ = a) ∗ l₁ ↦ a)",
        h:"The framed load, named before it is consumed. Preconditions agree; the two postconditions hold the same three conjuncts with the frame on opposite sides. The frame here is a single cell, so <code>preserves_of_heapOnly</code> covers it and the choice costs nothing."},
       {tac:'exact hoare_consequence (entails_refl _) step (star_comm _ _)',
        state:"No goals.",
        h:"One law. <code>star_comm</code> moves the frame from the right of the top-level <code>∗</code> to the left, which is exactly the difference the previous state showed."}
     ]},

    {t:'code', tag:'illustration', cap:'Rung one, complete. Four lines, and the only decision in it is the <code>(Q := …)</code> above.',
     src:"example (tmp₁ : Var) (l₁ l₂ : Loc) (a b : Val) :\n    Hoare ((l₁ ↦ a) ∗ (l₂ ↦ b)) (.load tmp₁ l₁)\n      ((l₂ ↦ b) ∗ (pure (fun σ => σ tmp₁ = a) ∗ (l₁ ↦ a))) := by\n  have step : Hoare ((l₁ ↦ a) ∗ (l₂ ↦ b)) (.load tmp₁ l₁)\n      ((pure (fun σ => σ tmp₁ = a) ∗ (l₁ ↦ a)) ∗ (l₂ ↦ b)) :=\n    hoare_frame (hoare_load tmp₁ l₁ a) (heapLocal_load tmp₁ l₁)\n      (preserves_of_heapOnly _ (heapOnly_pointsTo l₂ b))\n  exact hoare_consequence (entails_refl _) step (star_comm _ _)"},

    {t:'p', h:"The obvious thing to try instead is to skip the <code>star_comm</code> and take <code>A₁</code> to be the load rule&rsquo;s postcondition unchanged, <code>(pure φ₁ ∗ (l₁ ↦ a)) ∗ (l₂ ↦ b)</code>. Rung one then costs nothing at all. Rung two pays for it, and the way it pays is worth seeing, because the message names the rule that decided the shape:"},

    {t:'state', cap:'What the frame rule says when the assertion it is handed has the frame on the wrong side. The <code>?m.14</code> is the value in the pure fact, still undetermined because nothing in the mismatched types has pinned it down. <code>preserves_load_fact</code> is the discharger rung two needs; it is the next section&rsquo;s business, and nothing here turns on it.',
     src:"error: Type mismatch\n  hoare_frame (hoare_load tmp₂ l₂ b) (heapLocal_load tmp₂ l₂) (preserves_load_fact l₂ hne)\nhas type\n  Hoare (l₂ ↦ b ∗ _root_.pure fun σ => σ tmp₁ = ?m.14) (Cmd.load tmp₂ l₂)\n    (((_root_.pure fun σ => σ tmp₂ = b) ∗ l₂ ↦ b) ∗ _root_.pure fun σ => σ tmp₁ = ?m.14)\nbut is expected to have type\n  Hoare (((_root_.pure fun σ => σ tmp₁ = a) ∗ l₁ ↦ a) ∗ l₂ ↦ b) (Cmd.load tmp₂ l₂)\n    (((_root_.pure fun σ => σ tmp₂ = b) ∗ l₂ ↦ b) ∗ (_root_.pure fun σ => σ tmp₁ = a) ∗ l₁ ↦ a)"},

    {t:'p', h:"Read the two preconditions against each other. Whatever frame you give it, <code>hoare_frame</code>&rsquo;s precondition begins <code>l₂ ↦ b ∗ …</code>, because that is what the load rule owns; the assertion offered begins with a <code>pure</code>. The reshaping has to happen somewhere, and putting it in rung one&rsquo;s postcondition rather than rung two&rsquo;s precondition is a preference, not a saving."},

    /* ================================ the fact that survives a load ==== */

    {t:'sec', s:'A fact that has to survive a load'},

    {t:'p', h:"Rung two. The command is <code>.load tmp₂ l₂</code>, its footprint is <code>l₂ ↦ b</code>, and the frame is everything else in <code>A₁</code>: <code>pure φ₁ ∗ (l₁ ↦ a)</code>. The frame rule wants <code>Preserves (.load tmp₂ l₂)</code> of that, and the course has exactly two ways of producing a <code>Preserves</code>."},

    {t:'p', h:"Neither of them works. <code>preserves_of_heapOnly</code> asks the assertion never to consult the store, and half of this frame is a claim about the store — Unit 24 refuted that shape by name, in <code>not_heapOnly_pure</code>. <code>preserves_of_storeStable</code> asks the command not to touch the store, and the whole purpose of a load is to touch the store; Unit 29 refuted that with an explicit run. The two dischargers were built so that their gaps do not overlap, and this obligation sits in the overlap of the gaps."},

    {t:'p', h:"So the hypothesis has to mention both variables at once. The load writes <code>tmp₂</code>; the fact is about <code>tmp₁</code>; a function updated at one point still agrees with itself everywhere else. That is Unit 04&rsquo;s <code>update_other</code> in a new costume, and the disequality it needs is <code>tmp₁ ≠ tmp₂</code> — the hypothesis in <code>swap_spec</code> that has been sitting there unexplained."},

    {t:'note', kind:'key', title:'The store side of separation', h:"Two cells at distinct addresses do not interfere, and that is why the frame rule can carry a heap assertion across a write. Two <i>variables</i> with distinct names do not interfere either, and that is why a fact about one can be carried across a load into the other. The heap version is a hypothesis you obtain from a <code>∗</code> in the precondition; the store version is a hypothesis somebody has to write down, because there is no <code>∗</code> splitting the store. That asymmetry is the price of Unit 12&rsquo;s decision: an assertion is a claim about a store <i>and</i> a heap, but only the heap is what <code>∗</code> divides."},

    /* --------------------------------------------------------- m9-4 --- */

    {t:'ex',
     id:'m9-4',
     name:'preserves_load_fact',
     why:"This is the only ingredient the previous unit did not supply, and it is the reason <code>swap</code> is the capstone rather than a longer version of <code>copyCell</code>. It is also where the hypothesis <code>tmp₁ ≠ tmp₂</code> is spent on the compositional route: the whole proof is an inversion, an unfolding and a <code>simp</code> that fires on the disequality. Honest note: the specification shipped as <code>x62</code> takes the direct route and does not cite this theorem. What it buys you is the compositional route, which is on this page, measured, and is the route you would take on a program you cared about.",
     setup:"<code>Preserves c R</code> is Unit 24&rsquo;s. <code>pure φ</code> is <code>aAnd (fact φ) emp</code>, so its proofs are pairs. Inversion on the run, then <code>Store.set</code>&rsquo;s definition, is the whole proof.",
     goal:"theorem preserves_load_fact {x y : Var} {v : Val} (l : Loc) (hne : y ≠ x) :\n    Preserves (.load x l) (pure (fun σ => σ y = v)) := by",
     hints: [
       "Unfolded, the goal is: for every run of <code>.load x l</code> from <code>s</code> to <code>s'</code>, and every heap <code>hFrame</code>, if <code>pure (fun σ => σ y = v)</code> holds of <code>s.store</code> at <code>hFrame</code> then it holds of <code>s'.store</code> at <code>hFrame</code>. And <code>pure φ</code> is a conjunction: <code>φ</code> of the store, and the heap is empty.",
       "There is one rule of <code>Exec</code> whose conclusion is a run of a load, and it says what the final state is: the store updated at <code>x</code>, the heap untouched. So the goal becomes a claim about <code>Store.set s.store x _</code> evaluated at <code>y</code>. A function updated at <code>x</code> and read at <code>y</code>, with <code>y ≠ x</code>, is the original function at <code>y</code>. The heap half of the conjunction is unchanged and can be handed straight back.",
       "Five instruments. <code>intro</code>, to open the definition of <code>Preserves</code>. <code>cases … with | load …</code>, which is what replaces the opaque final state by an explicit one. <code>obtain</code>, and an anonymous constructor under a <code>refine</code>, to take the <code>pure</code> apart and put it back together, since only one of its two halves needs any work. <code>show</code> — needed because the store half is displayed as <code>fact</code> applied to a projection out of a structure literal, and until you write the equation out there is no <code>Store.set</code> in the goal for the next tactic to fire on. And <code>simp</code>, given <code>Store.set</code> and <code>hne</code>.",
       "<code>intro s s' hex hFrame hr</code> then <code>cases hex with | load hl =></code> leaves the goal <code>⊢ _root_.pure (fun σ => σ y = v) { store := s.store.set x v✝, heap := s.heap }.store hFrame</code>. After <code>obtain ⟨hy, he⟩ := hr</code> and <code>refine ⟨?_, he⟩</code> only the store half is left, and <code>show Store.set s.store x _ y = v</code> puts it in a form <code>simp [Store.set, hne]</code> can reduce to <code>⊢ s.store y = v</code>, which is <code>hy</code>."
     ],
     sol:"theorem preserves_load_fact {x y : Var} {v : Val} (l : Loc) (hne : y ≠ x) :\n    Preserves (.load x l) (pure (fun σ => σ y = v)) := by\n  intro s s' hex hFrame hr\n  cases hex with\n  | load hl =>\n      obtain ⟨hy, he⟩ := hr\n      refine ⟨?_, he⟩\n      show Store.set s.store x _ y = v\n      simp [Store.set, hne]\n      exact hy",
     solNote:"The underscore in the <code>show</code> is the value that was loaded. It has an inaccessible name — inversion produced it and you cannot type <code>v✝</code> — so the underscore is not laziness, it is the only way to write the term.",
     expl:"Invert the run to find out what the final store is, split the <code>pure</code> into its store half and its heap half, hand the heap half back untouched, and settle the store half by the fact that an update at <code>x</code> is invisible at <code>y</code>.",
     walk: [
       {tac:'intro s s\' hex hFrame hr', h:"Takes the two states, the run, the frame heap, and the assertion at the starting store. The goal is the same assertion at the finishing store, and nothing yet connects the two stores."},
       {tac:'cases hex with | load hl =>', h:"Inversion, and it eliminates <code>s'</code> rather than constraining it: <code>Exec.load</code>&rsquo;s conclusion fixes the final state as <code>{ store := s.store.set x v✝, heap := s.heap }</code>. Two new things appear — the loaded value <code>v✝</code>, inaccessible, and <code>hl</code>, the lookup that licensed the load. Neither is used again; what was needed was the shape of the final store."},
       {tac:'obtain ⟨hy, he⟩ := hr', h:"<code>pure φ</code> is <code>aAnd (fact φ) emp</code>, so the hypothesis is a pair: <code>hy</code> says <code>σ y = v</code> at the starting store, <code>he</code> says the frame heap is empty. Splitting it now means the two halves can be answered separately, and they are answered very differently."},
       {tac:'refine ⟨?_, he⟩', h:"The goal is a pair of the same shape. Its second component is about <code>hFrame</code> and the store does not occur in it, so <code>he</code> fits with nothing to prove; the hole is the first component. This is the step that reduces the theorem to a fact about <code>Store.set</code>."},
       {tac:'show Store.set s.store x _ y = v', h:"The goal is displayed as <code>fact (fun σ => σ y = v)</code> applied to a structure literal&rsquo;s <code>.store</code> field. That is definitionally the equation written here, and writing it out is what gives <code>simp</code> a <code>Store.set</code> to fire on. The underscore stands for the inaccessible loaded value."},
       {tac:'simp [Store.set, hne]', h:"<code>Store.set σ x w y</code> unfolds to <code>if y = x then w else σ y</code>, and <code>hne : y ≠ x</code> settles the condition, so the <code>else</code> branch is taken and the goal collapses to <code>⊢ s.store y = v</code>. This is the only line in the proof that uses the disequality, and it uses it as a <code>simp</code> lemma rather than as an argument."},
       {tac:'exact hy', h:"Which is the hypothesis, unchanged, at the store it was always about."}
     ],
     deep: [
       {t:'trace', title:'preserves_load_fact, every state',
        start:"x y : Var\nv : Val\nl : Loc\nhne : y ≠ x\n⊢ Preserves (Cmd.load x l) (_root_.pure fun σ => σ y = v)",
        steps:[
          {tac:'intro s s\' hex hFrame hr',
           state:"s s' : State\nhex : Exec (Cmd.load x l) s s'\nhFrame : Heap\nhr : _root_.pure (fun σ => σ y = v) s.store hFrame\n⊢ _root_.pure (fun σ => σ y = v) s'.store hFrame",
           h:"The four binder lines are unchanged from the start state and are omitted here and below. Goal and hypothesis differ in one subterm, the store, and <code>s'</code> is still opaque."},
          {tac:'cases hex with | load hl =>',
           state:"case load\ns : State\nhFrame : Heap\nhr : _root_.pure (fun σ => σ y = v) s.store hFrame\nv✝ : Val\nhl : s.heap l = some v✝\n⊢ _root_.pure (fun σ => σ y = v) { store := s.store.set x v✝, heap := s.heap }.store hFrame",
           h:"<code>s'</code> is gone from the context. The final store is now written out, and the loaded value has arrived as <code>v✝</code> — a name Lean will print and refuse to accept from you."},
          {tac:'obtain ⟨hy, he⟩ := hr  ·  refine ⟨?_, he⟩',
           state:"case load\ns : State\nhFrame : Heap\nv✝ : Val\nhl : s.heap l = some v✝\nhy : fact (fun σ => σ y = v) s.store hFrame\nhe : emp s.store hFrame\n⊢ fact (fun σ => σ y = v) { store := s.store.set x v✝, heap := s.heap }.store hFrame",
           h:"Two tactics, one state — the <code>obtain</code> only splits the hypothesis and the <code>refine</code> only drops the second component of the goal. What is left is the store half alone, and <code>he</code> was accepted without a rewrite because <code>emp</code> does not mention the store."},
          {tac:'show Store.set s.store x _ y = v',
           state:"⊢ s.store.set x v✝ y = v",
           h:"Context unchanged; only the goal line is shown. <code>fact φ σ h</code> is <code>φ σ</code> by definition, and projecting <code>.store</code> out of a literal reduces, so this is the same proposition written the way the next tactic needs to see it."},
          {tac:'simp [Store.set, hne]',
           state:"⊢ s.store y = v",
           h:"The update has disappeared. This is where <code>hne</code> is spent, and it is the entire mathematical content of the theorem."}
        ],
        done:"exact hy"},
       {t:'p', h:"Compare the three dischargers now that all of them exist. <code>preserves_of_heapOnly</code> takes a hypothesis about the assertion and ignores the command. <code>preserves_of_storeStable</code> takes a hypothesis about the command and ignores the assertion. This one takes a hypothesis relating the two — the variable the command writes against the variable the assertion reads — and that is why it is the one that could not be stated until there was a program with two temporaries in it."}
     ],
     pitfall:"Writing the hypothesis the other way round, <code>hne : x ≠ y</code>. The theorem is still true, and the proof still looks right, and <code>simp [Store.set, hne]</code> quietly fails to fire: the condition it meets is <code>y = x</code> and the lemma it is given is about <code>x = y</code>. The error surfaces on the <i>next</i> line, as <code>Type mismatch … but is expected to have type (if y = x then v✝ else s.store y) = v</code>, and Lean also reports <i>This simp argument is unused: hne</i> on the line above, which is the real diagnosis. The repair is one word: <code>simp [Store.set, Ne.symm hne]</code>.",
     variants:"Drop the disequality and the theorem is false, with a short witness: at <code>x = y = 0</code>, a store constantly <code>7</code> and a heap holding <code>5</code> at address <code>0</code>, the run <code>Exec.load (singleton_same 0 5)</code> takes <code>σ 0 = 7</code> to <code>σ 0 = 5</code>, so <code>¬ Preserves (.load 0 0) (pure (fun σ => σ 0 = 7))</code> is provable in five lines. Inside the proof above the loss is visible one line before the failure: with nothing to settle the branch, <code>simp [Store.set]</code> leaves <code>⊢ (if y = x then v✝ else s.store y) = v</code>, and the error arrives on the <code>exact hy</code> beneath it, where <code>hy</code> no longer has that type. The conditional standing there unreduced <i>is</i> the disequality&rsquo;s job. Replace <code>pure</code> by <code>fact</code> and the statement is still true and still proved by the same tactics — the <code>refine ⟨?_, he⟩</code> and the <code>obtain</code> both disappear, since there is no heap half. Replace the load by an assignment <code>.assign x e</code> and it is again true by the same argument, but the value stored is <code>e.eval s.store</code> instead of a heap lookup, so the inversion produces no <code>hl</code>."},

    /* ============================================ framing a compound ==== */

    {t:'p', h:"One obstacle is left on rung two, and it is not about stores at all. The frame is <code>pure φ₁ ∗ (l₁ ↦ a)</code>: two conjuncts, needing two different dischargers, and <code>Preserves</code> has no rule for <code>∗</code>. There is no combinator in this course that takes <code>Preserves c P</code> and <code>Preserves c R</code> to <code>Preserves c (P ∗ R)</code>."},

    {t:'p', h:"You do not need one. The frame rule can be applied twice, framing one conjunct at a time, and each application meets a frame it has a discharger for."},

    {t:'code', tag:'illustration', cap:'Rung two with two frames instead of one. The inner <code>hoare_frame</code> carries the store fact; the outer one carries the cell.',
     src:"example (tmp₁ tmp₂ : Var) (l₁ l₂ : Loc) (a b : Val) (hne : tmp₁ ≠ tmp₂) :\n    Hoare (((l₂ ↦ b) ∗ pure (fun σ => σ tmp₁ = a)) ∗ (l₁ ↦ a)) (.load tmp₂ l₂)\n      (((pure (fun σ => σ tmp₂ = b) ∗ (l₂ ↦ b)) ∗ pure (fun σ => σ tmp₁ = a)) ∗ (l₁ ↦ a)) :=\n  hoare_frame\n    (hoare_frame (hoare_load tmp₂ l₂ b) (heapLocal_load tmp₂ l₂)\n      (preserves_load_fact l₂ hne))\n    (heapLocal_load tmp₂ l₂)\n    (preserves_of_heapOnly _ (heapOnly_pointsTo l₁ a))"},

    {t:'p', h:"Read the precondition. Framing twice brackets it to the <i>left</i> — <code>((l₂ ↦ b) ∗ pure φ₁) ∗ (l₁ ↦ a)</code> — where framing once gives the right-nested <code>(l₂ ↦ b) ∗ (pure φ₁ ∗ (l₁ ↦ a))</code>. Every reshaping chain downstream then starts from a different bracketing, and the four rungs stop looking like four instances of one pattern. The combinator is four lines, two of them the statement, and it buys that uniformity back:"},

    {t:'code', tag:'illustration', cap:'What <code>Preserves</code> does to a <code>∗</code>: take the cut the assertion came with, carry each conjunct across the command at its own half of the heap, and put the cut back.',
     src:"theorem preserves_star {c : Cmd} {P R : Assertion}\n    (hp : Preserves c P) (hr : Preserves c R) : Preserves c (P ∗ R) := by\n  intro s s' hex hFrame ⟨h₁, h₂, hd, hu, h1, h2⟩\n  exact ⟨h₁, h₂, hd, hu, hp s s' hex h₁ h1, hr s s' hex h₂ h2⟩"},

    {t:'p', h:"Only the store moves. <code>Preserves</code> carries an assertion from one store to another with the heap argument held fixed, so the cut — the two heaps, their disjointness, the equation saying they union to the one in hand — makes no claim about the store and travels through untouched. What has to be re-established is the two conjuncts, and that is what the two hypotheses do, each at its own half. With that, every rung has the same five-line shape, and the compositional proof can be written out."},

    {t:'detail', title:'The whole program, verified by composition — 37 lines', tag:'aside', open:false, blocks:[
      {t:'p', h:"Three <code>hoare_seq</code> splits nested to the right, matching the program&rsquo;s brackets; four rungs, each a <code>have step</code> naming a framed small rule followed by a <code>hoare_consequence</code> whose postcondition side is a chain of <code>∗</code> laws. It compiles against this unit&rsquo;s context together with <code>preserves_star</code> above, and it is the proof the previous unit&rsquo;s method actually produces."},
      {t:'code', tag:'illustration',
       src:"theorem swap_by_composition (tmp₁ tmp₂ : Var) (l₁ l₂ : Loc) (a b : Val) (hne : tmp₁ ≠ tmp₂) :\n    Hoare ((l₁ ↦ a) ∗ (l₂ ↦ b)) (swap tmp₁ tmp₂ l₁ l₂) ((l₁ ↦ b) ∗ (l₂ ↦ a)) := by\n  refine hoare_seq (Q := (l₂ ↦ b) ∗ (pure (fun σ => σ tmp₁ = a) ∗ (l₁ ↦ a))) ?_ ?_\n  · have step : Hoare ((l₁ ↦ a) ∗ (l₂ ↦ b)) (.load tmp₁ l₁)\n        ((pure (fun σ => σ tmp₁ = a) ∗ (l₁ ↦ a)) ∗ (l₂ ↦ b)) :=\n      hoare_frame (hoare_load tmp₁ l₁ a) (heapLocal_load tmp₁ l₁)\n        (preserves_of_heapOnly _ (heapOnly_pointsTo l₂ b))\n    exact hoare_consequence (entails_refl _) step (star_comm _ _)\n  · refine hoare_seq (Q := (aAnd (fact (fun σ => σ tmp₂ = b)) (l₁ ↦ a))\n        ∗ (pure (fun σ => σ tmp₁ = a) ∗ (l₂ ↦ b))) ?_ ?_\n    · have step : Hoare ((l₂ ↦ b) ∗ (pure (fun σ => σ tmp₁ = a) ∗ (l₁ ↦ a))) (.load tmp₂ l₂)\n          ((pure (fun σ => σ tmp₂ = b) ∗ (l₂ ↦ b)) ∗ (pure (fun σ => σ tmp₁ = a) ∗ (l₁ ↦ a))) :=\n        hoare_frame (hoare_load tmp₂ l₂ b) (heapLocal_load tmp₂ l₂)\n          (preserves_star (preserves_load_fact l₂ hne)\n            (preserves_of_heapOnly _ (heapOnly_pointsTo l₁ a)))\n      refine hoare_consequence (entails_refl _) step ?_\n      refine entails_trans (star_assoc_left _ _ _) ?_\n      refine entails_trans (star_mono_right _ (star_swap_middle _ _ _)) ?_\n      refine entails_trans (star_mono_right _ (star_mono_right _ (star_comm _ _))) ?_\n      refine entails_trans (star_mono_right _ (star_swap_middle _ _ _)) ?_\n      exact pure_star_regroup _ _ _\n    · refine hoare_seq (Q := (aAnd (fact (fun σ => σ tmp₁ = a)) (l₂ ↦ b)) ∗ (l₁ ↦ b)) ?_ ?_\n      · have step : Hoare ((aAnd (fact (fun σ => (Atom.var tmp₂).eval σ = b)) (l₁ ↦ a))\n              ∗ (pure (fun σ => σ tmp₁ = a) ∗ (l₂ ↦ b)))\n            (.write l₁ (.var tmp₂))\n            ((l₁ ↦ b) ∗ (pure (fun σ => σ tmp₁ = a) ∗ (l₂ ↦ b))) :=\n          hoare_frame (hoare_write_val l₁ (.var tmp₂) a b) (heapLocal_write l₁ (.var tmp₂))\n            (preserves_of_storeStable (storeStable_write l₁ (.var tmp₂)) _)\n        refine hoare_consequence (entails_refl _) step ?_\n        refine entails_trans (star_swap_middle _ _ _) ?_\n        refine entails_trans (star_mono_right _ (star_comm _ _)) ?_\n        exact pure_star_regroup _ _ _\n      · have step : Hoare ((aAnd (fact (fun σ => (Atom.var tmp₁).eval σ = a)) (l₂ ↦ b)) ∗ (l₁ ↦ b))\n            (.write l₂ (.var tmp₁)) ((l₂ ↦ a) ∗ (l₁ ↦ b)) :=\n          hoare_frame (hoare_write_val l₂ (.var tmp₁) b a) (heapLocal_write l₂ (.var tmp₁))\n            (preserves_of_storeStable (storeStable_write l₂ (.var tmp₁)) _)\n        exact hoare_consequence (entails_refl _) step (star_comm _ _)"},
      {t:'p', h:"Three of the four <code>Preserves</code> obligations are discharged by a route the previous unit gave you and the fourth by <code>preserves_load_fact</code>. Eight of the thirty-five proof lines are the entailment chains — four <code>entails_trans</code> steps at the second rung, two at the third, and a <code>pure_star_regroup</code> closing each. No heap and no run is named anywhere in the proof; the only store in it is the <code>σ</code> bound inside the two facts, and nothing is ever proved about that either."}
    ]},

    /* ==================================================== two routes ==== */

    {t:'sec', s:'Two routes, measured'},

    {t:'p', h:"There is another way to prove <code>swap_spec</code>, and it is the one the corpus ships. Unfold <code>Hoare</code>, destructure the precondition into two singleton heaps, build the four-step <code>Exec</code> derivation by hand, and finish with one equation between heaps. It is the proof the previous unit told you to stop writing."},

    {t:'cmp',
     left:{t:'By composition', tag:'sketch',
       h:"Four rungs, each a framed small rule and a chain of <code>∗</code> laws. Thirty-seven lines, plus the four-line <code>preserves_star</code>. Eight lines are reshaping. Names no heap and no run, and the only store in it is the one bound inside a fact. No address is ever compared with another.",
       src:"refine hoare_seq (Q := A₁) ?_ ?_\n· have step := hoare_frame …\n  exact hoare_consequence …\n· refine hoare_seq (Q := A₂) ?_ ?_\n  · have step := hoare_frame …\n    refine entails_trans … (four laws)\n  · refine hoare_seq (Q := A₃) ?_ ?_\n    · …\n    · …"},
     right:{t:'From the semantics', tag:'sketch',
       h:"One heap lemma of seventeen lines and one triple of twenty-three. Names two singleton heaps, their union, a store updated twice, and an <code>Exec</code> derivation with four constructors in it. Compares <code>l₁</code> with <code>l₂</code> at four places, and needs to at every one.",
       src:"theorem swap_heap … := by\n  funext x\n  by_cases hx₂ : x = l₂ …\n\ntheorem swap_spec … := by\n  intro σ h hstar\n  obtain ⟨h₁, h₂, hd, hu, hp, hq⟩ := hstar\n  …\n  refine ⟨_, Exec.seq … , ?_⟩\n  …\n  exact swap_heap l₁ l₂ a b hlne"}},

    {t:'p', h:"Forty lines against forty-one. Whatever decides between these two routes, it is not length — and that is the first thing worth knowing, because length is what everyone reaches for."},

    {t:'p', h:"The compositional proof survives change. Add a fifth command and you add one rung; no existing rung has to be restated, because each of them is a statement about one command between two assertions and mentions neither its neighbours nor the rest of the program. Choose the new assertion so that the rung following it still fits — framing normally lets you — and the four already written are re-usable as they stand. The direct proof does not survive change at all: a fifth command means a fifth constructor threaded through the derivation and a different final heap, so <code>swap_heap</code> — the largest single piece of it — has to be restated and reproved."},

    {t:'p', h:"The direct proof is readable. <code>swap_heap</code> says, in one equation, what this program does to memory: write <code>b</code> at <code>l₁</code>, write <code>a</code> at <code>l₂</code>, and you have the two cells with their contents exchanged. Somebody who has never seen this course can check that claim. The eight lines of entailment chain in the compositional proof say nothing to anybody; they are a permutation of four conjuncts, correct and unreadable, and there is no way to look at them and see whether they are the right permutation short of running Lean."},

    {t:'note', kind:'key', title:'The rule', h:"Compose while the pieces you need are already theorems and the reshaping between them is mechanical — that is nearly always, and it is the only route that keeps the proof the same size as the program. Drop to the semantics when the program&rsquo;s effect on the whole heap is a single equation you can <i>state</i>, because then that equation is the proof, it is checkable by inspection, and it is the thing you would have wanted to write down anyway. What never justifies dropping to the semantics is that the composition is fiddly."},

    {t:'p', h:"By the previous unit&rsquo;s own test — <code>Exec</code> in your proof means you are missing a lemma — the direct route fails, and here the diagnosis is exact: the missing lemma is <code>preserves_star</code>, and it is four lines. The corpus ships the direct proof anyway, on the second ground rather than the first: <code>swap_heap</code> is a statement about memory that is worth having whether or not this program exists. That is a preference, stated as one; both proofs are on this page, and you can weigh them yourself."},

    /* ================================================ the heap lemma ==== */

    {t:'sec', s:'The heap computation, alone'},

    {t:'p', h:"Take the direct route and one equation is the whole of it. The precondition <code>(l₁ ↦ a) ∗ (l₂ ↦ b)</code> says the heap is <code>Heap.union (Heap.singleton l₁ a) (Heap.singleton l₂ b)</code>. The program writes <code>b</code> at <code>l₁</code> and then <code>a</code> at <code>l₂</code>. The postcondition says the result is <code>Heap.union (Heap.singleton l₁ b) (Heap.singleton l₂ a)</code>. Prove that before touching the triple, and the triple becomes assembly."},

    {t:'p', h:"It is an equation between functions, so it begins with <code>funext</code> and then splits the address space into regions: the two addresses the program touches, and everything else. Which region to take first is settled by the term rather than by taste. Each case split you resolve strips one operation off the left-hand side, so asking about the address of the <i>outermost</i> operation leaves a shorter term in both branches; ask about the inner one first and both writes are still standing when you get there. Here that also means the disequality <code>l₁ ≠ l₂</code> is needed in only one of the three regions — everywhere else the case split has already supplied a disequality of its own."},

    /* ---------------------------------------------------------- x61 --- */

    {t:'ex',
     id:'x61',
     name:'swap_heap',
     hard: true,
     why:"This is the mathematical content of the whole verification, extracted so that it can be proved without a triple, a store or a run anywhere near it. It is also the first pointwise heap argument in five units — the fragments for Units 26 to 29 contain no <code>funext</code> at all — and it is the three-region split <code>update_comm</code> had in Unit 04, over a union rather than a store. Get it standing and <code>x62</code> is assembly on top of it.",
     setup:"<code>funext</code>, <code>by_cases</code> and <code>subst</code>, and the interface: <code>write_same</code>, <code>write_other</code>, <code>singleton_same</code>, <code>singleton_other</code>, <code>union_of_some</code>, <code>union_of_none</code>. No <code>Heap.write</code> or <code>Heap.union</code> is ever unfolded.",
     goal:"theorem swap_heap (l₁ l₂ : Loc) (a b : Val) (hne : l₁ ≠ l₂) :\n    Heap.write (Heap.write (Heap.union (Heap.singleton l₁ a) (Heap.singleton l₂ b)) l₁ b) l₂ a\n      = Heap.union (Heap.singleton l₁ b) (Heap.singleton l₂ a) := by",
     hints: [
       "Both sides are heaps, and a heap is a function <code>Loc → Option Val</code>. The claim is that the two functions agree at every address. So the goal, after one tactic, is an equation between two <code>Option Val</code>s at an arbitrary <code>x</code>, and every address falls into one of three cases: <code>x = l₂</code>, <code>x = l₁</code>, or neither.",
       "In each region, evaluate both sides. At <code>l₂</code> the outer write answers <code>some a</code>, and the right-hand union misses in its left component and hits <code>some a</code> in its right. At <code>l₁</code> the outer write is not at <code>l₁</code> so it passes through, the inner write answers <code>some b</code>, and the right-hand union hits <code>some b</code> immediately. Elsewhere both writes pass through, both unions miss on the left, and both singletons at <code>l₂</code> miss too, so both sides are <code>none</code>.",
       "<code>funext x</code>, then <code>by_cases hx₂ : x = l₂</code>, and in the positive branch <code>subst hx₂</code> before rewriting. Then a nested <code>by_cases hx₁ : x = l₁</code> in the negative branch. Every step is a <code>rw</code> with one of the six interface lemmas; nothing else is needed and no <code>simp</code> appears anywhere.",
       "<code>funext x</code> leaves <code>⊢ (((Heap.singleton l₁ a).union (Heap.singleton l₂ b)).write l₁ b).write l₂ a x = (Heap.singleton l₁ b).union (Heap.singleton l₂ a) x</code>. After <code>by_cases hx₂ : x = l₂</code> and <code>subst hx₂</code>, <code>rw [write_same]</code> turns the left-hand side into <code>some a</code>, and the hypothesis in the context now reads <code>hne : l₁ ≠ x</code>, so the argument to <code>singleton_other l₁ x b</code> has to be <code>fun h => hne h.symm</code>."
     ],
     sol:"theorem swap_heap (l₁ l₂ : Loc) (a b : Val) (hne : l₁ ≠ l₂) :\n    Heap.write (Heap.write (Heap.union (Heap.singleton l₁ a) (Heap.singleton l₂ b)) l₁ b) l₂ a\n      = Heap.union (Heap.singleton l₁ b) (Heap.singleton l₂ a) := by\n  funext x\n  by_cases hx₂ : x = l₂\n  · subst hx₂\n    rw [write_same]\n    rw [union_of_none (Heap.singleton x a) (singleton_other l₁ x b (fun h => hne h.symm))]\n    rw [singleton_same]\n  · rw [write_other _ l₂ x a hx₂]\n    by_cases hx₁ : x = l₁\n    · subst hx₁\n      rw [write_same, union_of_some (Heap.singleton l₂ a) (singleton_same x b)]\n    · rw [write_other _ l₁ x b hx₁,\n          union_of_none (Heap.singleton l₂ b) (singleton_other l₁ x a hx₁),\n          union_of_none (Heap.singleton l₂ a) (singleton_other l₁ x b hx₁),\n          singleton_other l₂ x b hx₂, singleton_other l₂ x a hx₂]",
     solNote:"<code>fun h => hne h.symm</code> in the first branch is <code>Ne.symm hne</code> written out; the shorter spelling compiles too. Both branches of the second split end with a <code>rw</code> and no closing tactic, because <code>rw</code> tries <code>rfl</code> when it is done.",
     expl:"One <code>funext</code>, then three regions. In each region both sides are driven down to a value by rewriting with the lookup lemmas, and the two values agree. Nothing is unfolded and no equation about heaps is proved twice.",
     walk: [
       {tac:'funext x', h:"Turns an equation between two heaps into an equation between two <code>Option Val</code>s at an arbitrary address, and hands you the <code>x</code> to split on. Without it there is nothing to do a case analysis on."},
       {tac:'by_cases hx₂ : x = l₂', h:"The outermost operation on the left is the write at <code>l₂</code>, so the first question about <code>x</code> is whether it is that address. Taking the splits in the order the operations are nested keeps each branch to one layer at a time."},
       {tac:'subst hx₂', h:"Replaces <code>l₂</code> by <code>x</code> everywhere, including in <code>hne</code>, which now reads <code>l₁ ≠ x</code>. Substituting rather than rewriting means the write is literally at <code>x</code>, so <code>write_same</code> applies with no arguments to supply."},
       {tac:'rw [write_same]', h:"The left-hand side collapses from a two-layer write over a union to <code>some a</code>, in one step, without looking at anything underneath it. That is the payoff of stating the interface as lookup lemmas back in Unit 05."},
       {tac:'rw [union_of_none (Heap.singleton x a) (singleton_other l₁ x b (fun h => hne h.symm))]', h:"Now the right-hand side. <code>Heap.union h₁ h₂</code> is left-biased, so to get past its left component you must show that component misses. <code>singleton_other l₁ x b</code> needs <code>x ≠ l₁</code>; the context holds <code>l₁ ≠ x</code>; the lambda flips it. This is the one place the hypothesis is used in the entire proof."},
       {tac:'rw [singleton_same]', h:"The right-hand side is now <code>Heap.singleton x a x</code>, which is <code>some a</code>. Both sides agree, and the trailing <code>rfl</code> inside <code>rw</code> closes the branch without a further tactic."},
       {tac:'rw [write_other _ l₂ x a hx₂]', h:"The negative branch. <code>x</code> is not <code>l₂</code>, so the outer write passes through and one layer is gone. Two more regions remain underneath."},
       {tac:'by_cases hx₁ : x = l₁ · subst hx₁ · rw [write_same, union_of_some …]', h:"At <code>l₁</code> the inner write answers <code>some b</code>, and on the right the union hits its left component immediately, so <code>union_of_some</code> takes it to <code>some b</code> as well. Two rewrites in one bracket, because neither needs a state between them."},
       {tac:'rw [write_other …, union_of_none …, union_of_none …, singleton_other …, singleton_other …]', h:"The third region, five rewrites, all of them saying <i>this address is not that address</i>. The inner write passes through; both unions miss on the left; both remaining singletons are at <code>l₂</code> and <code>x</code> is not <code>l₂</code>. Both sides reach <code>none</code>."}
     ],
     deep: [
       {t:'trace', title:'The first region, x = l₂',
        start:"l₁ l₂ : Loc\na b : Val\nhne : l₁ ≠ l₂\n⊢ (((Heap.singleton l₁ a).union (Heap.singleton l₂ b)).write l₁ b).write l₂ a =\n    (Heap.singleton l₁ b).union (Heap.singleton l₂ a)",
        steps:[
          {tac:'funext x',
           state:"x : Loc\n⊢ (((Heap.singleton l₁ a).union (Heap.singleton l₂ b)).write l₁ b).write l₂ a x =\n    (Heap.singleton l₁ b).union (Heap.singleton l₂ a) x",
           h:"The three unchanged binder lines are omitted here and below. The only difference from the start state is the trailing <code>x</code> on each side — both sides are now values rather than functions."},
          {tac:'by_cases hx₂ : x = l₂  ·  subst hx₂',
           state:"case pos\nl₁ : Loc\na b : Val\nx : Loc\nhne : l₁ ≠ x\n⊢ (((Heap.singleton l₁ a).union (Heap.singleton x b)).write l₁ b).write x a x =\n    (Heap.singleton l₁ b).union (Heap.singleton x a) x",
           h:"The full context, because it changed: <code>l₂</code> has left the context entirely and <code>hne</code> has been rewritten to <code>l₁ ≠ x</code>. This is the line that decides the shape of the next rewrite&rsquo;s argument."},
          {tac:'rw [write_same]',
           state:"⊢ some a = (Heap.singleton l₁ b).union (Heap.singleton x a) x",
           h:"Four operations on the left, one rewrite, and the left-hand side is a value. The write at <code>x</code> read at <code>x</code> does not care what it was written over."},
          {tac:'rw [union_of_none (Heap.singleton x a) (singleton_other l₁ x b (fun h => hne h.symm))]',
           state:"⊢ some a = Heap.singleton x a x",
           h:"The union has gone. What remains is closed by <code>singleton_same</code>, and <code>rw</code>&rsquo;s trailing <code>rfl</code> does the rest."}
        ],
        done:"rw [singleton_same]"},
       {t:'trace', title:'The other two regions',
        start:"case neg\nl₁ l₂ : Loc\na b : Val\nhne : l₁ ≠ l₂\nx : Loc\nhx₂ : ¬x = l₂\n⊢ ((Heap.singleton l₁ a).union (Heap.singleton l₂ b)).write l₁ b x = (Heap.singleton l₁ b).union (Heap.singleton l₂ a) x",
        steps:[
          {tac:'(already applied) rw [write_other _ l₂ x a hx₂]',
           state:"case pos\nl₂ : Loc\na b : Val\nx : Loc\nhx₂ : ¬x = l₂\nhne : x ≠ l₂\n⊢ ((Heap.singleton x a).union (Heap.singleton l₂ b)).write x b x = (Heap.singleton x b).union (Heap.singleton l₂ a) x",
           h:"The start state above is what the outer write leaves. This is the state after <code>by_cases hx₁ : x = l₁</code> and <code>subst hx₁</code> — <code>l₁</code> is gone and <code>hne</code> reads <code>x ≠ l₂</code>. Both sides are now about the address the inner write touched, and two rewrites finish it."},
          {tac:'(the neg branch) rw [write_other …, union_of_none …, union_of_none …]',
           state:"case neg\nl₁ l₂ : Loc\na b : Val\nhne : l₁ ≠ l₂\nx : Loc\nhx₂ : ¬x = l₂\nhx₁ : ¬x = l₁\n⊢ Heap.singleton l₂ b x = Heap.singleton l₂ a x",
           h:"Three regions in, and what is left is the cleanest statement of why the third one is trivial: neither singleton is being read at its own address, so both answer <code>none</code> and it does not matter that their values differ. The last two rewrites say exactly that."}
        ],
        done:"rw [singleton_other l₂ x b hx₂, singleton_other l₂ x a hx₂]"},
       {t:'p', h:"Count the appeals to the disequality. In the whole proof there is one, in the first region, and it is needed because at <code>l₂</code> the right-hand union has to be shown to miss on its left. In the second region <code>hx₂</code> does that job and in the third region <code>hx₁</code> and <code>hx₂</code> do it twice each. A theorem with a hypothesis used once is a theorem whose hypothesis you should be able to point at, and here you can."}
     ],
     pitfall:"Passing <code>hne</code> straight to <code>singleton_other</code> in the first branch. After <code>subst hx₂</code> the hypothesis reads <code>l₁ ≠ x</code> and the lemma wants <code>x ≠ l₁</code>, so Lean reports <i>Application type mismatch: The argument hne has type l₁ ≠ x but is expected to have type x ≠ l₁</i>. It is easy to read that as a mistake about which of <code>l₁</code> and <code>l₂</code> you are in, when it is only about which side of a <code>≠</code> the substitution left them on. <code>hne.symm</code> or <code>fun h => hne h.symm</code> repairs it; both compile.",
     variants:"Drop <code>hne</code> and the theorem is false. At <code>l₁ = l₂ = 0</code>, <code>a = 1</code>, <code>b = 2</code>: the union is left-biased so the initial heap is <code>Heap.singleton 0 1</code>, the two writes leave <code>Heap.singleton 0 1</code>, and the right-hand side is <code>Heap.singleton 0 2</code>. Four tactics refute the universally quantified form — instantiate at those values, take <code>congrFun</code> of the result at address <code>0</code>, drive both sides down with <code>write_same</code> and <code>union_of_some</code>, and <code>cases</code> the <code>h : some 1 = some 2</code> that is left. Swap the order of the two writes on the left — write <code>a</code> at <code>l₂</code> first, then <code>b</code> at <code>l₁</code> — and the equation stops needing <code>hne</code> at all: delete the hypothesis from the statement and it still goes through. The write that lands last is now the one at <code>l₁</code>, and the union on the right is left-biased, so at <code>l₁</code> both sides answer <code>b</code> whether or not <code>l₁</code> and <code>l₂</code> are the same address. In the original order the last write is at <code>l₂</code> and answers <code>a</code> exactly where the left-biased union answers <code>b</code>, and that single disagreement is the whole of what <code>hne</code> rules out. The proof has to be rearranged region by region as well, since the case split follows the new outermost write and so asks <code>x = l₁</code> first — and the one appeal to <code>hne</code> becomes an appeal to <code>hx₁</code>. Reverse the equation and it is the same theorem; the proof is unchanged, since every step is a rewrite of one side down to a value."},

    /* ================================================== the capstone ==== */

    {t:'p', h:"What is left is to get from the triple to that equation. Three things stand between them: the precondition has to be taken apart, the four-step run has to be built, and the goal Lean displays at the end is nothing like the goal you want to prove."},

    {t:'p', h:"The first is where <code>l₁ ≠ l₂</code> comes from. Destructuring <code>(l₁ ↦ a) ∗ (l₂ ↦ b)</code> yields two heaps, a proof that they are disjoint, and the information that each is a singleton. Substitute the singletons in and the disjointness hypothesis becomes <code>Heap.disjoint (Heap.singleton l₁ a) (Heap.singleton l₂ b)</code>, which Unit 08 proved is equivalent to <code>l₁ ≠ l₂</code>. So the caller supplies the disequality, in the only form a caller can — by owning two cells separately — and the theorem never has to ask for it."},

    {t:'p', h:"The second is the run. Four commands means a derivation with four constructors in it, and each of them wants a lookup: <code>Exec.load</code> needs the address it reads to be mapped, and <code>Exec.write</code> needs the address it writes to be mapped too, since this language will not write where nothing is allocated. There are only three distinct lookups to prove. Both commands that touch <code>l₁</code> — the first load and the first write — run on the same starting heap, so one equation serves both. The command that touches <code>l₂</code> last runs after a write at <code>l₁</code>, and <code>write_other</code> takes it straight back to the lookup the second load already used. And the nesting of <code>Exec.seq</code> has to match the nesting of <code>;;</code>, which is to the right."},

    {t:'p', h:"The third is a matter of display. After the run is supplied the goal is the postcondition applied to a state built from four nested structure literals, and it prints at <b>383 lines</b>. Cutting the postcondition&rsquo;s <code>∗</code> takes it to 192, which is no improvement you can read. Nothing is wrong with either display; every projection in them reduces. But <code>rw</code> matches the term as it stands, so <code>rw [e1]</code> reports <i>Did not find an occurrence of the pattern</i> and prints the whole target expression back at you. The tool for this is <code>show</code>: state the equation you know the goal is, Lean checks it definitionally, and what is left is three lines."},

    /* ---------------------------------------------------------- x62 --- */

    {t:'ex',
     id:'x62',
     name:'swap_spec',
     hard: true,
     why:"The capstone. Four commands, two temporaries, no hypothesis about the addresses, and every ingredient already on the page or already in your editor. This is also the one exercise in the first edition of this course that shipped without a verified solution; what you are about to write closes it.",
     setup:"<code>swap_heap</code> is available. So is <code>singleton_disjoint_iff</code> (Unit 08), <code>singleton_disjoint</code> (Unit 08), <code>union_of_some</code> and <code>union_of_none</code> (Unit 09), <code>write_other</code> (Unit 05). The proof unfolds <code>Hoare</code> and builds an <code>Exec</code> derivation by hand; that is deliberate here and is argued for above.",
     goal:"theorem swap_spec (tmp₁ tmp₂ : Var) (l₁ l₂ : Loc) (a b : Val) (hne : tmp₁ ≠ tmp₂) :\n    Hoare ((l₁ ↦ a) ∗ (l₂ ↦ b)) (swap tmp₁ tmp₂ l₁ l₂) ((l₁ ↦ b) ∗ (l₂ ↦ a)) := by",
     hints: [
       "Unfolded, the goal asks for a state <code>s'</code>, a run of <code>swap</code> reaching it, and a proof that <code>(l₁ ↦ b) ∗ (l₂ ↦ a)</code> holds there. The precondition is a <code>∗</code>, so it gives you two heaps, their disjointness, their union, and the fact that each is a singleton. Substituting the singletons everywhere makes the starting heap a closed term you can look things up in.",
       "Four commands, four premises. Each <code>Exec.load</code> needs a lookup in the heap it runs on, and each <code>Exec.write</code> needs the same. There are only three distinct lookups to establish: <code>l₁</code> and <code>l₂</code> in the initial union, and <code>l₂</code> in the heap after the first write — the last of which reduces to the second because the write was at <code>l₁</code>. The two store values the writes need, <code>tmp₁</code> and <code>tmp₂</code> read after two updates, are two more small equations, and one of them is where <code>tmp₁ ≠ tmp₂</code> is used.",
       "<code>intro</code>, <code>obtain</code> the six slots of the <code>∗</code>, three <code>subst</code>s, then <code>(singleton_disjoint_iff a b).mp hd</code> for the disequality, then a <code>have</code> for each lookup and each store value, then one <code>refine</code> supplying the run, one <code>refine</code> supplying the <code>∗</code> in the postcondition, one <code>show</code>, one <code>rw</code>, and <code>swap_heap</code>.",
       "<code>intro σ h hstar</code> then <code>obtain ⟨h₁, h₂, hd, hu, hp, hq⟩ := hstar</code> then <code>subst hp; subst hq; subst hu</code>. Those three substitutions are what turn <code>hd</code> into a statement about two singletons, so that <code>have hlne : l₁ ≠ l₂ := (singleton_disjoint_iff a b).mp hd</code> typechecks. The run is <code>Exec.seq (Exec.load hH1) (Exec.seq (Exec.load hH2) (Exec.seq (Exec.write hH1) (Exec.write hH3)))</code> — right-nested, matching the program."
     ],
     sol:"theorem swap_spec (tmp₁ tmp₂ : Var) (l₁ l₂ : Loc) (a b : Val) (hne : tmp₁ ≠ tmp₂) :\n    Hoare ((l₁ ↦ a) ∗ (l₂ ↦ b)) (swap tmp₁ tmp₂ l₁ l₂) ((l₁ ↦ b) ∗ (l₂ ↦ a)) := by\n  intro σ h hstar\n  obtain ⟨h₁, h₂, hd, hu, hp, hq⟩ := hstar\n  subst hp; subst hq; subst hu\n  have hlne : l₁ ≠ l₂ := (singleton_disjoint_iff a b).mp hd\n  have hH1 : Heap.union (Heap.singleton l₁ a) (Heap.singleton l₂ b) l₁ = some a :=\n    union_of_some _ (singleton_same l₁ a)\n  have hH2 : Heap.union (Heap.singleton l₁ a) (Heap.singleton l₂ b) l₂ = some b := by\n    rw [union_of_none (Heap.singleton l₂ b) (singleton_other l₁ l₂ a (Ne.symm hlne)), singleton_same]\n  have e2 : Store.set (Store.set σ tmp₁ a) tmp₂ b tmp₂ = b := by simp [Store.set]\n  have e1 : Store.set (Store.set σ tmp₁ a) tmp₂ b tmp₁ = a := by simp [Store.set, hne]\n  have hH3 : Heap.write (Heap.union (Heap.singleton l₁ a) (Heap.singleton l₂ b)) l₁\n      (Store.set (Store.set σ tmp₁ a) tmp₂ b tmp₂) l₂ = some b := by\n    rw [write_other _ l₁ l₂ _ hlne.symm]; exact hH2\n  refine ⟨_, Exec.seq (Exec.load hH1) (Exec.seq (Exec.load hH2)\n      (Exec.seq (Exec.write hH1) (Exec.write hH3))), ?_⟩\n  refine ⟨Heap.singleton l₁ b, Heap.singleton l₂ a, singleton_disjoint b a hlne, ?_, rfl, rfl⟩\n  show Heap.write (Heap.write (Heap.union (Heap.singleton l₁ a) (Heap.singleton l₂ b)) l₁\n        (Store.set (Store.set σ tmp₁ a) tmp₂ b tmp₂)) l₂\n        (Store.set (Store.set σ tmp₁ a) tmp₂ b tmp₁) = _\n  rw [e1, e2]\n  exact swap_heap l₁ l₂ a b hlne",
     solNote:"Twenty-one lines of proof, ten of them <code>have</code>s establishing one small equation each, three of them the <code>show</code>, and one the citation of <code>swap_heap</code>. The final state is written <code>_</code> in the first <code>refine</code> because the derivation determines it — supplying it by hand would mean writing out the four nested structure literals that print at 383 lines.",
     expl:"Take the precondition apart until the heap is a closed term; read the disequality off the disjointness; establish the five small equations the four <code>Exec</code> premises and the two writes need; supply the derivation; cut the final heap as two singletons; and reduce what is left to <code>swap_heap</code>.",
     walk: [
       {tac:'intro σ h hstar', h:"Unfolds <code>Hoare</code>. The goal is an existential over states: a run of <code>swap</code> and the postcondition at wherever it lands."},
       {tac:'obtain ⟨h₁, h₂, hd, hu, hp, hq⟩ := hstar', h:"The six slots of a <code>∗</code>: two heaps, disjointness, the union equation, and the two conjuncts. The last two are equations, since <code>l ↦ v</code> is defined as <i>the heap is this singleton</i>."},
       {tac:'subst hp; subst hq; subst hu', h:"Three substitutions in one line, and after them the heap in the goal is the closed term <code>Heap.union (Heap.singleton l₁ a) (Heap.singleton l₂ b)</code>, with <code>h</code>, <code>h₁</code> and <code>h₂</code> gone from the context. Everything after this depends on the heap being closed: a lookup in a variable heap cannot be computed."},
       {tac:'have hlne : l₁ ≠ l₂ := (singleton_disjoint_iff a b).mp hd', h:"<code>hd</code> was a statement about two variables; after the substitutions it is a statement about two singletons, which is what <code>singleton_disjoint_iff</code> is about. This is the whole answer to <i>where does the disequality come from</i>: the caller gave it, in the act of separating the two cells."},
       {tac:'have hH1 : … l₁ = some a := union_of_some _ (singleton_same l₁ a)', h:"The first lookup. The union is left-biased and the left component hits, so one appeal to <code>union_of_some</code>. This equation is used twice — once by the first load and once by the first write, which read the same address in the same heap."},
       {tac:'have hH2 : … l₂ = some b := by rw [union_of_none …, singleton_same]', h:"The second lookup goes past the left component, so it needs that component to miss: <code>singleton_other l₁ l₂ a</code> with <code>Ne.symm hlne</code>, since the lemma wants <code>l₂ ≠ l₁</code> and the context has the other orientation."},
       {tac:'have e2 : Store.set (Store.set σ tmp₁ a) tmp₂ b tmp₂ = b := by simp [Store.set]', h:"The store after two updates, read at the variable most recently written. No disequality needed: <code>simp</code> settles <code>tmp₂ = tmp₂</code> by itself."},
       {tac:'have e1 : Store.set (Store.set σ tmp₁ a) tmp₂ b tmp₁ = a := by simp [Store.set, hne]', h:"The same store read at the <i>other</i> variable, which has to see past the outer update. This is the one line in the proof that uses <code>tmp₁ ≠ tmp₂</code>, and it is the direct route&rsquo;s version of <code>preserves_load_fact</code>&rsquo;s single <code>simp</code>."},
       {tac:'have hH3 : … = some b := by rw [write_other _ l₁ l₂ _ hlne.symm]; exact hH2', h:"The fourth command reads <code>l₂</code> in the heap the third command left. That write was at <code>l₁</code>, so <code>write_other</code> passes straight through it and the lookup is <code>hH2</code> again. The underscore is the written value, which does not matter and would be tedious to write."},
       {tac:'refine ⟨_, Exec.seq (Exec.load hH1) (Exec.seq (Exec.load hH2) (Exec.seq (Exec.write hH1) (Exec.write hH3))), ?_⟩', h:"The witness and the run. The nesting is right-associated to match <code>swap</code>&rsquo;s: <code>c₁ ;; (c₂ ;; (c₃ ;; c₄))</code>, so the derivation is <code>seq _ (seq _ (seq _ _))</code>. The final state is left as <code>_</code> and Lean computes it from the derivation."},
       {tac:'refine ⟨Heap.singleton l₁ b, Heap.singleton l₂ a, singleton_disjoint b a hlne, ?_, rfl, rfl⟩', h:"The postcondition is a <code>∗</code>, so proving it means choosing the cut. Both halves are singletons, they are disjoint by <code>hlne</code> again, and the two <code>rfl</code>s are the two <code>↦</code> conjuncts. The remaining hole is the union equation — the claim that those two singletons really are the heap the program produced."},
       {tac:'show Heap.write (Heap.write … ) l₂ (Store.set (Store.set σ tmp₁ a) tmp₂ b tmp₁) = _', h:"The hole is displayed as a 192-line term built from projections out of nested structure literals. Every projection reduces, so this <code>show</code> is accepted, and it is the difference between a goal you can work with and one you cannot. The trailing <code>_</code> is the right-hand side, which was already readable."},
       {tac:'rw [e1, e2]', h:"Replaces the two store expressions by the values they compute to. This is why the <code>show</code> had to come first: <code>rw</code> matches the printed term, and before the <code>show</code> the store was printed as <code>{ store := σ, heap := … }.store.set tmp₁ a</code> rather than <code>σ.set tmp₁ a</code>, so the pattern was not there to find."},
       {tac:'exact swap_heap l₁ l₂ a b hlne', h:"And the goal is now, character for character, the lemma from the previous exercise."}
     ],
     deep: [
       {t:'trace', title:'From the destructured precondition to swap_heap',
        start:"tmp₁ tmp₂ : Var\nl₁ l₂ : Loc\na b : Val\nhne : tmp₁ ≠ tmp₂\nσ : Store\nh h₁ h₂ : Heap\nhd : h₁.disjoint h₂\nhu : h = h₁.union h₂\nhp : (l₁ ↦ a) σ h₁\nhq : (l₂ ↦ b) σ h₂\n⊢ ∃ s', Exec (swap tmp₁ tmp₂ l₁ l₂) { store := σ, heap := h } s' ∧ (l₁ ↦ b ∗ l₂ ↦ a) s'.store s'.heap",
        steps:[
          {tac:'subst hp; subst hq; subst hu',
           state:"tmp₁ tmp₂ : Var\nl₁ l₂ : Loc\na b : Val\nhne : tmp₁ ≠ tmp₂\nσ : Store\nhd : (Heap.singleton l₁ a).disjoint (Heap.singleton l₂ b)\n⊢ ∃ s',\n    Exec (swap tmp₁ tmp₂ l₁ l₂) { store := σ, heap := (Heap.singleton l₁ a).union (Heap.singleton l₂ b) } s' ∧\n      (l₁ ↦ b ∗ l₂ ↦ a) s'.store s'.heap",
           h:"Four hypotheses have gone and the heap is closed. <code>hd</code> is now about two singletons, which is the only form <code>singleton_disjoint_iff</code> accepts."},
          {tac:'refine ⟨_, Exec.seq (Exec.load hH1) (Exec.seq (Exec.load hH2) (Exec.seq (Exec.write hH1) (Exec.write hH3))), ?_⟩',
           state:"⊢ (l₁ ↦ b ∗ l₂ ↦ a)\n    {\n        store :=\n          {\n              store :=\n                {\n                    store :=\n                      {\n                              store :=\n                                { store := σ, heap := (Heap.singleton l₁ a).union (Heap.singleton l₂ b) }.store.set tmp₁\n                                  a,\n                              heap :=",
           h:"The context is unchanged and omitted, and so are the remaining <b>371</b> lines of the goal: this is the first twelve of a display 383 lines long, cut off where it happened to reach, with nothing rearranged. Four commands&rsquo; worth of structure literals nested inside each other, one layer per command, every earlier layer reappearing inside every later one. It is correct, it reduces, and nothing in it can be read."},
          {tac:'refine ⟨Heap.singleton l₁ b, Heap.singleton l₂ a, singleton_disjoint b a hlne, ?_, rfl, rfl⟩',
           state:"⊢ {\n        store :=\n          {\n              store :=\n                {\n                    store :=\n                      {\n                              store :=\n                                { store := σ, heap := (Heap.singleton l₁ a).union (Heap.singleton l₂ b) }.store.set tmp₁\n                                  a,\n                              heap :=\n                                { store := σ,",
           h:"The first twelve lines again, of a display now 192 lines long. The two <code>rfl</code>s discharged the two <code>↦</code> conjuncts against the two singletons supplied, and what is left is one equation between heaps — with the program&rsquo;s final heap on the left, printed as the state it was projected out of."},
          {tac:'show Heap.write (Heap.write (Heap.union …) l₁ (Store.set (Store.set σ tmp₁ a) tmp₂ b tmp₂)) l₂ (Store.set (Store.set σ tmp₁ a) tmp₂ b tmp₁) = _',
           state:"⊢ (((Heap.singleton l₁ a).union (Heap.singleton l₂ b)).write l₁ ((σ.set tmp₁ a).set tmp₂ b tmp₂)).write l₂\n      ((σ.set tmp₁ a).set tmp₂ b tmp₁) =\n    (Heap.singleton l₁ b).union (Heap.singleton l₂ a)",
           h:"Three lines, in full, and this one is real rather than elided. 192 lines to 3, by asserting what the goal is and letting Lean check it. Every projection in the old display was <code>{ store := σ, heap := … }.store</code> and reduced to <code>σ</code>; the <code>show</code> is the reader doing that reduction on paper."},
          {tac:'rw [e1, e2]',
           state:"⊢ (((Heap.singleton l₁ a).union (Heap.singleton l₂ b)).write l₁ b).write l₂ a =\n    (Heap.singleton l₁ b).union (Heap.singleton l₂ a)",
           h:"The stores are gone. This is <code>swap_heap</code>&rsquo;s statement, and the last line of the proof is the citation."}
        ],
        done:"exact swap_heap l₁ l₂ a b hlne"},
       {t:'p', h:"Look at the two goals that were elided. Neither of them is hard, and neither of them is even <i>different</i> from the three-line goal that follows — they are the same proposition, printed without the reductions performed. This is the clearest instance in the course of the two-column rule: what is being proved is three lines long, and what Lean requires in order to prove it is a <code>show</code>."}
     ],
     pitfall:"Leaving out the three <code>subst</code>s and going straight for the disequality. The heap is still the variable <code>h</code>, the two components are still <code>h₁</code> and <code>h₂</code>, and <code>(singleton_disjoint_iff a b).mp hd</code> reports <i>Application type mismatch: The argument hd has type h₁.disjoint h₂ but is expected to have type (Heap.singleton l₁ a).disjoint (Heap.singleton l₂ b)</i>. The same omission then breaks every lookup as well, since <code>Exec.load hH1</code> wants a fact about <code>{ store := σ, heap := h }.heap</code>. The second common one is bracketing the derivation to the left, <code>Exec.seq (Exec.seq …) …</code>, which produces three errors at once, the first of them <i>Application type mismatch … has type Exec (Cmd.load ?m ?m ;; Cmd.load ?m ?m) … but is expected to have type Exec (Cmd.load tmp₁ l₁) …</i>. <code>;;</code> is <code>infixr</code>; the derivation has to nest the same way.",
     variants:"Drop <code>hne</code> and the specification is false: with <code>tmp₁ = tmp₂</code> the second load overwrites the value the first one saved, so the last write puts <code>b</code> into <code>l₂</code> and the cells end up holding <code>b</code> and <code>b</code>. The proof fails at <code>e1</code>, which is where the hypothesis is spent, and it fails legibly: the goal <code>simp</code> cannot close is <code>⊢ tmp₁ = tmp₂ → b = a</code>, which is the counterexample written out as an obligation. Add <code>l₁ ≠ l₂</code> as a hypothesis and the theorem is still true and one line shorter — the <code>have hlne</code> goes — but the specification is weaker in the sense that matters: a caller now has to prove something before calling, where before the precondition proved it for them. Change the postcondition to the false <code>(l₁ ↦ b) ∗ (l₂ ↦ b)</code>, and with it the second witness in the second <code>refine</code> and that <code>refine</code>&rsquo;s disjointness argument, which becomes <code>singleton_disjoint b b hlne</code> — leave the argument alone and you get a second, earlier error there, saying it proves disjointness of <code>Heap.singleton l₂ a</code> where <code>Heap.singleton l₂ b</code> is wanted. Change all three and the proof breaks at the last line and nowhere else: one <i>Type mismatch</i> saying <code>swap_heap</code>&rsquo;s right-hand side is <code>(Heap.singleton l₁ b).union (Heap.singleton l₂ a)</code> where the goal wants <code>… (Heap.singleton l₂ b)</code>. The derivation, the six <code>have</code>s and the <code>show</code> all go through — a fair measure of how much of this proof is about the run and how little about the answer."},

    /* ================================================ retrospective ==== */

    {t:'sec', s:'Retrospective'},

    {t:'p', h:"Three questions. If the answers are not to hand, the folds have them, but try them first."},

    {t:'detail', title:'Where did l₁ ≠ l₂ come from?', tag:'aside', open:false, blocks:[
      {t:'p', h:"From the precondition, through <code>singleton_disjoint_iff</code>. A caller who can produce <code>(l₁ ↦ a) ∗ (l₂ ↦ b)</code> owns two cells on disjoint heaps, and two singleton heaps are disjoint exactly when their addresses differ. The disequality was never a side condition on the rule; it was a consequence of the resources the caller handed over."},
      {t:'p', h:"That is the whole difference between this specification and the one Unit 00 could not write down. There, the disequality had to be written into the rule and discharged by every caller. Here it is written nowhere: the direct proof reads it off the precondition in one term, and the compositional proof does not mention addresses at all, because the separating is done by the frame rule and the disjointness is discharged inside <code>hoare_frame</code>, once, where you cannot see it."}
    ]},

    {t:'detail', title:'Which Preserves discharger did each command need, and why did they differ?', tag:'aside', open:false, blocks:[
      {t:'p', h:"All three routes appear in one four-command program. Rung one frames a bare cell past a load: the frame never consults the store, so <code>preserves_of_heapOnly</code>. Rungs three and four frame assertions that <i>do</i> consult the store past writes: the command never touches the store, so <code>preserves_of_storeStable</code>. Rung two has a frame that reads the store and a command that writes it, so neither applies and <code>preserves_load_fact</code> is what carries it — plus <code>preserves_star</code>, or a second application of the frame rule, to get past the fact that the frame has two conjuncts."},
      {t:'p', h:"The pattern behind the three: the obligation is about an interaction between a command and an assertion, and you can kill an interaction by fixing either end or by ruling it out at both. Fix the assertion — it does not read the store. Fix the command — it does not write the store. Or leave both moving and show they move in different places, which is what a disequality between variables says."}
    ]},

    {t:'detail', title:'What would change if the program had five commands instead of four?', tag:'aside', open:false, blocks:[
      {t:'p', h:"For the compositional proof: one more <code>hoare_seq</code>, one more assertion, one more rung of the same five-line shape. The four rungs already written need no rewriting, because each of them is a claim about one command between two assertions and is blind to what surrounds it. That invariance — proof length tracking program length rather than heap size — is the practical argument for this entire subject, and it is the thing the frame rule bought."},
      {t:'p', h:"For the direct proof: everything after the fifth command changes. A fifth <code>Exec</code> constructor has to be threaded into the derivation with its own premise; the final heap gains another layer; the <code>show</code> gains a line; and <code>swap_heap</code>, seventeen lines of it, has to be restated for the new expression and reproved region by region. Seventeen of the direct proof&rsquo;s forty lines have to be written again for a program one command longer; the compositional proof&rsquo;s forty-one stay as they are and gain five."}
    ]},

    {t:'dod', h:"Given a straight-line program and a specification, you can write down the assertion between each pair of commands before opening an editor, by reading the program backwards and asking what the next command&rsquo;s rule needs. You can arrange each assertion so that the frame rule cuts it in the right place, and recognise the type mismatch that says you did not. You can discharge a <code>Preserves</code> obligation three ways and say which applies. You can carry a fact about one program variable across a command that writes another, and point at the line where the disequality is spent. You can extract a program&rsquo;s effect on memory as a standalone heap equation and prove it by regions. You can build an <code>Exec</code> derivation whose bracketing matches its program, use <code>show</code> to make an unreadable goal readable, and say why <code>rw</code> needed you to. And given a program, you can choose between the two routes and defend the choice."},

    {t:'p', h:"Four intermediate assertions, all chosen by hand and all forced by what the <i>next</i> command needed. That is a strong hint they can be computed backwards from the postcondition — and they can."}

  ]
});
